# Branches and pull requests

Read this before creating a branch, a commit or a pull request, in a clone or in the meta-repository.

## Clones

In every clone `main` is production and the rest branch is the test server. The rest branch is the clone's `ref` in `repos.toml`, usually `dev`; below it is called `dev`. No clone gets direct commits or pushes to `main`. A clone stays on its rest branch at rest: `mise run clone` creates a new clone on it, and `mise run pull` updates the meta-repository and each clone on its current branch and never switches branches. `git pull` also updates `origin/main`.

Each clone follows one of two branch models, recorded as `branch_model` in `repos.toml`. They differ in where a work branch starts and ends.

<!-- repos:begin -->

No repositories yet. `mise run add-repo` adds a row for each repository.

<!-- repos:end -->

Work branches are named `{{ feature_prefix }}<name>` for features and `{{ bugfix_prefix }}<ticket-or-name>` for fixes.

### Main-based model

| Branch | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `main` | Production. Receives work branches by pull request after QA approves them. |
| `dev` | Test server: `main` plus everything in QA. An integration branch, not a release branch. |
| `{{ feature_prefix }}<name>`, `{{ bugfix_prefix }}<ticket-or-name>` | Created from an updated `main`; reaches `dev` for QA by pull request; its own pull request goes to `main`. |
| `merge/<name>-into-dev` | Created from `dev` only when the work branch conflicts with `dev`; carries the pull request into `dev`. |

The order of work on a task:

1. `mise run pull` and `mise run status`: the clone is on `dev` with no foreign changes, and `origin/main` is fresh.
2. `git -C repos/<clone> switch --no-track -c {{ bugfix_prefix }}<name> origin/main`. `--no-track` keeps `origin/main` from becoming the branch's upstream; the agent's git guard refuses a bare `git push` whose upstream is `main`.
3. Edits, tests and checks with `mise run <clone>:*`. `mise run check` must be green before QA.
4. Commit and push the branch with `git push -u origin <branch>`.
5. Open a pull request from the work branch into `dev` for QA. A work branch is never merged into `dev` directly with `git merge`; it reaches `dev` only through a pull request, so every change on the test server has been reviewed.
6. After QA approves, open a pull request from the work branch into `main` (not from `dev`), as described in [ticket-workflow.md](ticket-workflow.md). It is merged with a merge commit, not squash or rebase.

The developer merges both pull requests by hand.

Never merge `dev` into a work branch and never branch from `dev`: it drags every unreleased change into the pull request to `main`. To pick up fresh code, `main` is merged into the branch, by the developer or on the developer's request. After a release, the developer merges `main` back into `dev`.

#### Conflicts with dev

When the pull request of a work branch into `dev` has conflicts, create a merge branch from `dev` named `merge/<work branch without its prefix>-into-dev`, for example `merge/PROJ-123-into-dev` for `{{ bugfix_prefix }}PROJ-123`. Merge the work branch into it, resolve the conflicts there, push it and open the pull request into `dev` from the merge branch. The pull request into `main` still comes from the work branch, which stays free of `dev`. If the merge branch already exists, reuse it.

#### Fixes after review or QA

Fixes after review or QA are committed on the same work branch and pushed, so the open pull request updates. When the work branch has a merge branch, merge the work branch into it again and push it. No second pull request is opened. Nothing is committed directly on a merge branch or on `dev`: a merge branch receives only merges of its work branch, so the fix also reaches `main` with the work branch.

### Dev-based model

| Branch | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `main` | Production. Receives only merges of `dev`. |
| `dev` | Test server. |
| `{{ feature_prefix }}<name>`, `{{ bugfix_prefix }}<ticket-or-name>` | Created from an updated `dev`; its pull request goes to `dev`. |

The order of work on a task:

1. `mise run pull` and `mise run status`: the clone is on `dev` with no foreign changes.
2. `git -C repos/<clone> switch -c {{ feature_prefix }}<name>`.
3. Edits, tests and checks with `mise run <clone>:*`.
4. Commit and push the branch, open a pull request to `dev` ([ticket-workflow.md](ticket-workflow.md)). Fixes after review or QA go on the same branch.

### Several clones

A task spanning several clones gets same-named branches in each, each following its own clone's model: a main-based branch starts from `main`, a dev-based branch from `dev`.

## Meta-repository

The meta-repository has one long-lived branch, `main`. A working branch is created from an updated `main`, and changes reach `main` only through a pull request. An OpenSpec change that touches the clones also gets a branch in the meta-repository, for its artifacts.

## Who merges

The agent commits, pushes and opens pull requests, as far as its settings allow ([agent-settings.md](agent-settings.md)). It never merges a pull request and never merges into `main`, `dev` or a rest branch, in any clone or the meta-repository. The developer merges pull requests by hand, because a merge into `dev` or `main` changes a server or production. The only merge the agent makes is a work branch into its merge branch.

## No Claude attribution

Commit messages, pull request titles, pull request descriptions and pull request comments carry no Claude attribution, in the clones and in the meta-repository: no `Co-Authored-By:` line naming Claude or `anthropic.com`, no `Generated with Claude Code` line and no other mention of Claude or Anthropic. The work goes out under the developer's name. Rule file names such as `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/` and the tool name `Claude Code` are allowed. The shared settings turn Claude Code's attribution lines off ([agent-settings.md](agent-settings.md)).

## Commit messages

Commit messages follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org): `feat: ...`, `fix: ...`, `docs: ...`, `chore: ...`, written in {{ language_name }} after the type.

## What code checks

- The agent does not commit or push without permission, and never commits or pushes to `main`, neither in the clones nor in the meta-repository ([agent-settings.md](agent-settings.md)).
- The agent's git hook refuses `gh pr merge` in every repository and `git merge` while the target repository is on `main`, `dev` or its rest branch.
- The agent's git hook refuses a commit or pull request text with Claude attribution and names what it found.
- The clones' own hooks run on a commit in a clone the same way as when working with the repository directly.
